{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Change 1717 got LLM-Review +1 with 5 advisory (non-blocking) findings. I fixed all 5 rather than landing on the +1 — two were real defects and one was a test of mine that overclaimed.\n\n1. (correctness) REAL BUG: 'compact-all --dry-run performs a real push when nothing is selected'. I put _best_effort_push on the total_needs == 0 early return, BEFORE the dry_run guard, and it only checked no_commit. So a dry run with nothing selected delivered whatever was pending. A dry run must have no side effects, and pushing is a side effect even when this invocation committed nothing of its own. _best_effort_push now takes keyword-only no_commit and dry_run and opts out of both. Added tests for --dry-run and --no-commit never pushing.\n\n2. (tests) REAL, and the finding I am most glad of: test_two_independent_folds_of_one_ticket_converge never MERGED the two clones. It folded in both and asserted each still reduced correctly — which proves nothing about the race its own docstring claimed to test. Two sweeps racing are on different machines and never see each other's locks, so convergence is a property of the union merge (I9), not of exclusion. A test that skips the merge tests nothing.\n   Rewrote it to actually fetch and merge clone B into clone A, and ran the experiment first to learn the real behaviour rather than assume it: each fold writes a SNAPSHOT under its own unique filename and renames the SAME sources to *.retired; git merges the identical renames as one change and both snapshots as adds; the merge is CLEAN (ort) and the ticket ends up carrying TWO snapshots, which the reducer tolerates and replays to identical state. The test now asserts exactly that — clean merge, both snapshots present, retired sources intact, same comment set.\n\n3. (tests) The new best-effort push path was untested — which is pointed, because that path exists precisely because I broke it invisibly. Added a test that a sweep folding nothing still pushes, and one that the 'Nothing to do' early return still pushes.\n\n4. (error-handling) My _best_effort_push docstring claimed pure best-effort, but push_after_commit deliberately re-raises OptionalDependencyError so a misconfigured S3 remote halts loudly. Corrected the docstring to state the fail-closed escape and why it is preserved: silencing it would make the one unrecoverable configuration error invisible on the store's only standing trigger.\n\n5. (iac) The workflow declared no timeout-minutes, inheriting GitHub's 6-hour default while reconcile-bridge.yml and _build-and-test.yml both bound at 60. Added timeout-minutes: 60 with the rationale that a truncated sweep is harmless — compaction is optional and the next run re-folds what is pending.\n\nGates re-verified: lint (needed a ruff format pass on the rewritten test), mypy 523 files, complexity baseline, wall-clock-assert, actionlint + zizmor clean. 57 tests across the affected modules pass. Amended onto the same Change-Id; full tiers re-running before I re-push."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786610424180050001,"uuid":"1553e23c-e733-4180-83d8-a2d79a8108ef"}